 "footnotes": [
  "p. 11 n. 1 (on à, l. 11): 'An interesting variant of this passage occurs on the leather roll, British Museum No. 10,473; it runs :—' then prints a parallel text in hieroglyphs, 2 rows.",
  "p. 12 n. 1 (on śenit, l. 13): 'In the papyrus of Hunefer (British Museum No. 9901) this chapter ends with the words, \"the Shenit, in the presence of the great god, lord of Amenta,\" but in several others, all of a later date, the chapter has here the words :—' then 2 rows of hieroglyphs with transliteration and translation ('the Shenit who make men and women to be in stability. Pleasant [is it] for us pleasant [is it] to hear gladness of heart at the weighing of words.'); then 'Here follows the petition, \"let not lies be spoken (hieroglyphs equals hieroglyph) against me near the great god, in the presence of the great god, lord of Amenta,\" and the chapter ends with,' 1 row of hieroglyphs: māk θenθ - k un - θà em maāχeru, '\"Verily how great wilt thou be rising up in triumph!\"'",
  "p. 12 n. 2 (on āḥāu - s, legend l. 15): 'Compare the legend in the papyrus of Ànhai (British Museum No. 10,472) :—' then prints the legend in hieroglyphs, 2 rows (contains a (?)).",
  "p. 15 n. 1 belongs to the Appendix (attached to χennu, out of range): 'Compare [hieroglyphs], Naville, Todtenbuch, Bd. II., Bl. 99.'",
  "p. 15 n. 2 (on Teḥuti, l. 16): 'In British Museum papyrus No. 9901, the speech of Thoth reads :—' then 2 rows of hieroglyphs with transliteration and translation: māk - à ḥer metru ren en Àusàr suten ān Hunefer àu àb - f pert ḥer māχa bu qem - nef t'ai, 'Verily I am justifying the name of Osiris the royal scribe Hunefer. His heart hath come forth upon the Scale, not hath he been found an evil doer.'",
  "p. 16 n. 1 (on χemennu, l. 26): 'The variants are' followed by four hieroglyphic spellings of the name (with N35, X1, Z7, O49 town signs)."
